 {
  "coord": "16:30",
  "verdict": "FAIL",
  "category": "grammar",
  "note": "\"misfit\" is not a verb here; source means \"was unbecoming to\".",
  "replacement": "CHIEF JUSTICE. I was then acting in your father's person. The image of his power lay in me then. And while I was busy administering his law for the good of the commonwealth, your Highness chose to forget my office, the majesty and power of law and justice, and the image of the King whom I represented, and struck me in my very seat of judgment. For that, as an offender against your father, I gave bold way to my authority and committed you to prison. If the deed was wrong, then answer this, now that you wear the garland: would you be content to have a son of yours set your decrees at nothing? To pull justice down from your awesome bench? To trip up the course of law and blunt the sword that guards the peace and safety of your own person? Even more, to kick at your most royal image and mock your actions as they are carried out in a second body? Question your royal thoughts and make the case your own. Be the father now, and picture a son. Hear your own dignity so profaned, see your most dreadful laws so loosely brushed aside, watch yourself so scorned by a son. Then imagine me taking your part and, in your power, quietly silencing your son. After this cool consideration, pass sentence on me. And as you are a king, say with royal authority what I did that was unbecoming to my office, my person, or my king's sovereignty."
 },
